module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.check_reachable_only_from_an_entry_point_the_context_never_calls

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { RecurringFailureMode }

data check_reachable_only_from_an_entry_point_the_context_never_calls: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "check_reachable_only_from_an_entry_point_the_context_never_calls" as NonEmptyStr,

  receipts: [
    "**a check exists, executes correctly, and is reachable only from ONE entry point of the tool -- and the context holding its subject never calls that entry point** (THE SUBJECT IS FULLY PRESENT IN THE EXECUTED POPULATION AND EVERY OTHER CHECK FIRES OVER IT. Nothing is unscheduled, nothing is unprepared, no denominator is misread. The missing edge is that the one check lives behind a subcommand the consumers of that context do not invoke, so for that population the wall is not weak, it is ABSENT -- while every signal a reader consults reports green.)",

    "THE INVALID STATE is a rule whose enforcing pass is bound to one invocation mode, in a context whose every consumer uses another. The harm is that violations accumulate on a tree indefinitely with review and CI both green. CI's GREEN IS CORRECT AND SAYS NOTHING ABOUT THE CLASS -- no mechanism it ran executes the check, so its silence is structural rather than an oversight. REVIEW'S GREEN IS NOT THEREBY VINDICATED, and the two must not be merged: a human reader could have caught the class by eye, or by invoking the compiling entry point, so what the missing invocation explains is why the class is INVISIBLE TO MECHANISM, not why it survived a reading. Merging them turns a missing wall into nobody's omission, which is exactly the excuse a ledger row must not manufacture. THE TELL is that the rule is enforced somewhere, so a grep for the checker finds it and a reader concludes it is covered; the question that decides it is not does the check exist but WHICH ENTRY POINTS EXECUTE IT, AND DOES THIS CONTEXT CALL ONE.",

    "SPECIMEN ONE, ONE TOOL BLIND (2026-09-12, still-raven-427, gunbc#11194). The DESIGN section 4c annotation-attachment check runs at gunbc compile. claim_batch resolves and EXECUTES the same modules without it. A lane held 155 GREEN WITNESSES that were true and structurally blind to an unattached trailing annotation, which then redded public CI at compile. The witnesses were not wrong and were not weak; they could not see the class at all, because the pass that sees it is not on the path claim_batch takes.",

    "SPECIMEN TWO, AN ENTIRE REPOSITORY WITH NO CALLER (2026-09-12, quick-owl-739, reported out of gunbc-private#102 and deliberately NOT bundled into it on blast-radius grounds). gunbc-private carries two workflows; between them the gunbc binary is invoked EXACTLY ONCE, as gunbc run, and never as gunbc compile. So section 4c has NO executing consumer anywhere in that repository. A compile over the private composed root reports 19 BLOCKING 4c errors, all in one file, and the count matches the 19 indented annotation lines that file carries on main -- attributable, not a compiler artifact.",

    "**THE EVIDENCE THAT MAKES THIS A CLASS RATHER THAN AN OVERSIGHT, AND IT IS THE PART WORTH KEEPING.** The file carrying those 19 violations arrived through gunbc-private#89: SIX REVIEW ROUNDS -- four REQUEST_CHANGES across three providers, one unresolved verdict, one APPROVE on the final head -- with both lanes green at merge. Nineteen blocking violations rode in anyway. THE RECORDED EXECUTIONS DID NOT PERFORM THE CHECK, which is a fact about what ran rather than about anyone's care: the lanes that executed invoked only the entry point that omits the pass. A reviewer COULD have read the annotations by eye or invoked the compiling entry point, and none did; what the evidence establishes is that no mechanism in that repository executed the check, not that no one could have caught it. A reader told only that 19 violations were found concludes somebody was sloppy; a reader told that NOTHING WHICH RAN WAS CAPABLE OF SEEING THE CLASS understands why the invocation is the fix and the annotations are the symptom. The narrower phrasing is the load-bearing one and the wider one was this receipt's own first landing: 'nothing COULD execute the check' would excuse the READING as well as the machinery, and the evidence supports only that nothing DID.",

    "**THE REPAIR IS A JOIN, NOT A FIX TO THE CHECK.** Every context that hosts a check's subject must invoke a consumer that EXECUTES that check, and the join -- check, to the entry points that execute it, to the contexts holding its subject -- is itself the thing that should be decidable and gated. Moving the pass into the shared resolve path closes it at the tool level; adding the missing invocation closes it per context. Note which is cheaper WHEN: the measured violation population at discovery was exactly one file, so the wall cost nineteen annotation moves that day. A missing wall only gets more expensive, and the cheapest moment to install one is the moment you discover it is missing.",

    "**AND THE WALL MUST BE OBSERVED TO REFUSE BEFORE IT COUNTS.** Installing the invocation is not the evidence; a discriminating red is -- re-introduce one violation in a throwaway overlay, show the new invocation catches it, revert. This class exists precisely because a check nobody executed was cited as coverage for an entire repository, which makes it the sharpest available argument that a wall never seen to refuse is a decoration.",

    "THE FOUR NEIGHBOURS THIS IS NOT, each located by where it puts the missing edge. [[witness_module_absent_from_the_executed_set]]: its check is a claim module on disk and the missing edge is SCHEDULE MEMBERSHIP, repaired by enrolling it; here there is nothing to enrol, because the check is a built-in pass and the subject is already in the executed set. [[preparation_membership_derived_from_the_execution_selector]]: there the module never enters the prepared population at all, so the WHOLE floor goes unchecked over it; here the module is prepared, resolved and executed and every other check fires -- its repair, a run deriving its own preparation coverage, would report full coverage and stay blind. [[green_reported_over_a_population_the_instrument_does_not_own]]: two actuators both EXECUTING, splitting one population, with a denominator mis-read; here no actuator for the check executes in that context at all, so no half is adjudicated by anyone. [[required_lens_red_control_never_executes_from_its_home]]: a join between an obligation census and an exclusion roster, about the evidence for a rung claim; here the join is between a check's host subcommand and the subcommands a context actually invokes.",

    "SPECIMEN THREE DOES NOT EXTEND THIS ROW'S RECOGNITION RULE, AND THE RULE IS QUOTED RATHER THAN RESTATED. The load-bearing sentence is `THE SUBJECT IS FULLY PRESENT IN THE EXECUTED POPULATION AND EVERY OTHER CHECK FIRES OVER IT`. gunbc#11194 at d75c000465d7ec26bc4b86fd28f212c7a776193d fails that premise: `test.claim.fabric.common_paper_csa_agreement_witness` was discovered by a newline scan of `test fn ` / `test data ` (`floor_discovery_scan_test_decl_names`) and was ABSENT from the Strict-prepared keep set (measured full_inventory=5601, prepared_inventory=2843). Infer never ran over `accepted_of`. That is the neighbour this row already names as [[preparation_membership_derived_from_the_execution_selector]], not a widening of `every other check fires`. What it shares with this row is the invocation shape: `v1.compiler.infer` `check_match_exhaustiveness` fires under `gunbc compile --entry` and under Strict prepare of the keep set, and the required-floor context does not invoke that pass over the 14943 `not_executed` identities (run 34740787182: discovered 18747, executed 3804). A green disposition cannot tell `resolved and fine` from `never resolved`.",

    "TYPE-GROWTH INVISIBILITY IS WHY A DIFF-KEYED SELECTOR CANNOT SUBSTITUTE FOR THAT INVOCATION. The class is not `a non-exhaustive match slipped through`. The class is that a match goes stale when its coproduct grows in ANOTHER module, so the affected file can have no diff at all and is invisible to any selector keyed on the diff, however good. `accepted_of` was exhaustive 10/10 when it landed. Arms grew later on `gunbc.product.fabric.provider_admission` `ProviderUseRefusal`. Improving `edited_test_fns` does not see a file the diff does not name. REFUSED `required_gate_prefixes` WIDENING: adding `test.claim.fabric.` (or this one module) clears one file and hides the invocation hole for the next file outside the gate -- the absorbing fallback, not a repair.",

    "ONE-SITE BOUND, AND ONLY THAT SCOPE. Among modules that NAME `ProviderUseRefusal` at d75c000, compiled `--entry` rather than grepped: one stale match, one `NonExhaustiveMatch` diagnostic, at `accepted_of:158` (missing PostureJurisdictionsUnread, TransferOrLocationUnresolved, ProviderAccessPathUnsupported). The other four consumers (`provider_admission_witness_test` `refusal_tag`/`refusal_located`, in-module `refusal_reason`, `grant_issue.dag`, `selection.dag`) compiled clean -- they had been updated with the type. THAT NUMBER BOUNDS THAT COPRODUCT'S NAMED CONSUMERS AT THAT COMMIT. It says the lane that grew the type mostly did its job and one site was missed. It says nothing about other coproducts that have grown, which is the actual class. Quoting `one site` without that frame is a misread of the census as a class size.",

    "THE ZERO THAT WAS REFUSED, NAMED AS THE TEACHING EXAMPLE. A whole-corpus `gunbc compile` without `--entry` refused at `WholeCorpusCompileBudgetBelowMeasuredDemand` (declared-unverified 8GiB against measured demand 16GiB). Infer never started. The log contained zero `NonExhaustiveMatch` lines. That zero was AVAILABLE, TRUE as a count of lines in a log, and reporting it would have closed `the corpus currently has zero` in the census's favour with nobody able to tell. It was refused: a zero from a run that never typechecked is the killed-process memorial the admission refusal exists to prevent -- absent versus never-looked, applied to this instrument rather than to someone else's code. The specimen shows a checker that never ran; this zero shows the false green you get when you read its silence as a result.",

    "THE UNMEASURED WHOLE-CORPUS COMPILE IS A DEPENDENCY WITH AN OWNER, NOT A PERMANENT CEILING. The admission refusal is `declared-unverified 8GiB` against measured demand 16GiB. nimble-badger-98 owns whole-corpus compile admission; gunbc#11227 (`Whole-corpus compile admission: demand is a fact of the root compiled (MeasuredForRoot | UnmeasuredRoot)`) is the path: demand must be a fact of the root compiled rather than a population-blind constant, and the self-host plan names the row instead of the number. `whole-tree compile is not the oracle on this runner` is that outstanding item, not a wall.",

    "THE DUPLICATEMODULE PLANT WAS REFUSED AS A DISCRIMINATOR, AND THAT REFUSAL IS THE INSTRUMENT. Two files claiming one module panic in `insert_module_path` over the FULL index BEFORE the keep filter, so it is a corpus-wide detector that would red-line even with a narrow compiled subject. It would have looked like a spanning subject while answering a different question -- this thread's failure mode, caught in the probe, against the more dramatic answer. Strict-prepare SIGKILL of the keep set was reported and was not allowed to reverse membership: membership is what answers whether the unplanned witness is in the compiled subject, whether or not that compile completes.",

    "A WRITTEN COVERAGE CLAIM ABOUT THIS NEIGHBOUR WAS FALSIFIED BY THE SAME MEASUREMENT. `gunbc.bare_name_fork_lens` header asserts that `the floor's whole-corpus preparation contains both` declarers of a forked bare name. There is no whole-corpus preparation (2843 of 5601). That face is prose about a neighbour mechanism, so it lives on [[stale_claim_survives_its_own_correct_edit]] rather than as a widening of this row's recognition rule. The lens itself is unharmed: it reads `v2.std.decl_index` `decl_facts` pool roots, not the prepared subject. Term (b) splits there: top-level `fn`/`type`/`data`/`service` are covered corpus-wide by that lens; variant arms are a declared bound on `bare_name_fork_lens_coverage_bound`, with next-rung trigger `variant constructors carried as their own decl facts`. Not built.",
  ],

  evidence: [],
}
